{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Whoever gives of his seed to Molekh.</b> After explaining the divine intent to sanctify Israel so that they might resemble Him as far as possible, and instructing them in the way by which the end intended by El, may He be exalted, is attained, and warning against its opposite — He then speaks of the punishment of those who defile themselves through one of three kinds of impurity that oppose the aforementioned holiness. The first is impurity in opinions, in the matter of Molekh, concerning which He said: לְמַעַן טַמֵּא אֶת מִקְדָּשִׁי (Leviticus 20:3); and in the matter of the ovot and yid'anim, concerning whom He said above: אַל תְּבַקְשׁוּ לְטָמְאָה בָהֶם (Leviticus 19:31). The second is impurity in seed, namely the forbidden relations, concerning which He said above: אַל תִּטַּמְּאוּ בְכָל אֵלֶּה, כִּי בְכָל אֵלֶּה נִטְמְאוּ הַגּוֹיִם וַתִּטְמָא הָאָרֶץ (Leviticus 18:24–25). The third is impurity in forbidden foods, concerning which He said at the end of this portion: אֲשֶׁר הִבְדַּלְתִּי לָכֶם לְטַמֵּא (Leviticus 20:25). However, in the portion of Emor He spoke of impurity through the dead, the profanation of seed, blemishes, and sacrilege — matters that pertain solely to consecrated things. He began by speaking of Molekh and said: <b>The people of the land shall stone him with stones.</b> This the multitude does out of zeal for the honor of their Maker — when there are witnesses and warning."], :book :leviticus, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:37:07.576099881Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}